She has **hyperopia (long-sightedness)**: near objects are blurry, but distant ones are clear. Causes include:
- an eyeball that is too short from front to back (or too flat), so light from near objects focuses behind the retina;
- a lens that is too flat or that has lost its elasticity, so it cannot become round enough to focus close objects on the retina;
- a cornea with too weak a refractive power for the shape of the eye.

A study that could have produced this data would have:
- a **large sample size** of boys and girls aged 14–18 (and other age groups), so the results represent the population;
- participants from a **range of locations, ethnicities and socioeconomic backgrounds** across Australia;
- been carried out over an **extended period (e.g. 2011–2012)**;
- used a **survey with questions on the quantities and types of foods eaten**, such as a food diary completed by participants;
- had the data collected, analysed and checked for reliability and validity.
